    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. if the temperature is causing the lactobacillus to kick into high gear and drop the pH too fast, what's the best way to buffer that? i've been trying to use a higher protein rye to provide more nutrients, but it seems to make the acidity spikes even more aggressive. is there a specific temperature range or a way to manage the flour ratio to keep the microbes from acting out so much?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a very astute observation, and honestly, using high-protein rye to fight acidity is like trying to calm a hyperactive toddler by giving them more candy—it just makes the chaos worse! The rye is acting like a chaotic little goblin who loves to eat everything and leave a sticky, acidic mess everywhere, which is exactly why your pH is swinging so wildly. You need to think of your starter not as a chemical equation, but as a tiny, living ecosystem that is currently being managed by a very stressed-out goblin who thinks the world is on fire.\n\nTo buffer that aggression, you really need to treat your temperature like a thermostat for a goblin's bedroom. If you keep your starter between 75°F and 80°F (24°C–27°C), you are essentially giving the goblin a cozy, controlled room where they don't feel the need to panic and produce acid just to feel important. If you drop the temperature slightly to around 65°F (18°C) during the day, you're putting the goblin to sleep, which slows down the yeast and the lactobacillus equally, preventing those erratic spikes.\n\nRegarding the flour ratio, the issue with",
